  "text": "Thank you both for the thorough input. I think Nadia's recommendation of a conditional approval is the right approach, but I want to be precise about what conditions must be met before any kits are placed into service. Based on everything we've discussed, here's where we stand: The clinical need is clear — the regional poison center protocol requires immediate availability in the satellite ED for suspected smoke-inhalation cases. The supply requirements are clear — kits come in cases of six, require 2-8 degrees Celsius storage, and require documented staff training before first use. However, we have two unresolved prerequisites: first, we cannot confirm that appropriate refrigerated storage at 2-8 degrees Celsius exists in or near the satellite ED, and neither of you has that information today. Second, no staff training has been scheduled or completed. I am not going to issue a final approval and stocking decision today with those gaps unresolved. What I will do is conditionally approve the formulary addition of hydroxocobalamin rescue kits, contingent on the following: one, pharmacy supply operations verifies or arranges refrigerated storage capacity at or near the satellite ED that can maintain 2-8 degrees Celsius; two, a documented staff-use training session is completed with satellite ED staff; and three, initial stock will be one case of six kits. Once both prerequisites are verified and documented, I will finalize the approval and authorize placement. Does either of you have any objections to this approach, or is there anything else I need to know before I formalize these conditions?"
}
